---
// src/pages/_site-updates/2026-10-04-founders-tour-booked-confirmation.astro
export const updateMeta = {
  date: '2026-10-04',
  title: 'Founders tour booking ends on a confirmation card',
  tags: ['waitlist', 'lead-capture', 'founders', 'tour'],
};
---
<article class="border-l-4 border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] pl-6">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">October 4, 2026</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
    After a Founders applicant books their tour on
    <a href="/pricing" class="text-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:underline">/pricing</a>, the booking
    calendar is replaced by a confirmation card that shows the tour time and what happens next.
  </p>

  <div class="mb-4">
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Why</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
      The embedded calendar's own confirmation was a large, mostly empty white card with a "Book
      another meeting" button, which is wrong for an applicant, and the page still showed the
      "Calendar not showing?" and "No time that works?" lines. The application ended without a
      clear sign that it was finished.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How it works</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
      When the calendar reports the booking (a message accepted only from the calendar's own
      origin and from the frame the page created), the frame is removed and the two fallback
      lines are hidden. A Your Tour Is Booked card, in the page's dark styling, takes its place.
      It shows the booked date and time in the applicant's own time zone, with the zone named,
      when the booking message carries a usable time, and leaves that line out otherwise. It
      explains that the calendar invite with the Zoom link comes from BreederHQ Founders
      (admin@breederhq.com) with both founders on it, that the invite's link is how to
      reschedule or cancel, and that joining from a computer is best. A Back to BreederHQ Home
      button ends the page. Focus moves to the card's heading for screen readers, and the card
      is scrolled into view without animation for visitors who prefer reduced motion. The
      fallback path that opens the booking page in a new tab is unchanged.
    </p>
  </div>
</article>
